@*
    Something an AI wrote, held for review.

    Separate from the act that produced it because the two have different
    lifetimes. The act starts the work and is finished the moment it returns; the
    proposal is the result and outlives it, sitting in the document until a
    person decides. A sixth state on IntegrationActionSpec would have tied the
    suggestion to a button that has already moved on.

    Nothing is applied in place. .design/design-principles.md#ai-first-surfaces
    requires AI actions that modify content to be reversible, and the cheap
    reading of that is an undo stack; this takes the strict one, where the change
    does not happen until somebody says so. Original and Body render as "Now" and
    "Suggested", and rejecting leaves the document byte-identical rather than
    restored — which are not the same thing, because a restore is a second edit
    and this is no edit at all.

    Two carriers for one fact, on purpose. The principles ask that AI content be
    visually distinguishable *and* labelled for assistive technology, and those
    are different requirements: a tinted edge is invisible to a screen reader and
    an aria-label is invisible to everyone else, so the card carries both.

    Accept is Primary and Reject is Ghost — never Danger. Discarding a suggestion
    destroys nothing: the document is exactly as it was, and painting the refusal
    red would make declining a machine's offer feel like deleting something.
*@

<article class="@ClassValue" aria-label="@($"Suggested by {ProviderName}")" data-testid="@TestId">
    @* The attribution outlives the decision. Accepting sets the state and keeps
       this line, because six months later "did a person write this paragraph" is
       a question the document has to be able to answer — which is the whole
       difference between provenance and a confirmation dialog. *@
    <p class="ai-proposal__attribution" data-testid="@AttributionTestId">
        <ProviderMark Provider="@Proposal.Provider" CssClass="ai-proposal__mark" />
        <span class="ai-proposal__provider">@ProviderName</span>

        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Proposal.Model))
        {
            <span class="ai-proposal__model">@Proposal.Model</span>
        }

        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Proposal.Timestamp))
        {
            <span class="ai-proposal__when">@Proposal.Timestamp</span>
        }

        @if (Proposal.State is not AiProposalState.Proposed)
        {
            <span class="ai-proposal__state">@StateLabel</span>
        }
    </p>

    @if (ShowOriginal && !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Proposal.Original))
    {
        <div class="ai-proposal__original" data-testid="@OriginalTestId">
            <span class="ai-proposal__caption">Now</span>
            <p class="ai-proposal__text">@Proposal.Original</p>
        </div>
    }

    <div class="ai-proposal__body" data-testid="@BodyTestId">
        <span class="ai-proposal__caption">Suggested</span>
        <p class="ai-proposal__text">@Proposal.Body</p>
    </div>

    @if (Proposal.State is AiProposalState.Proposed)
    {
        <div class="ai-proposal__actions">
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Primary"
                       Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                       TestId="@AcceptTestId"
                       OnClick="() => OnAccept.InvokeAsync(Proposal)">@AcceptLabel</AppButton>

            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                       Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                       TestId="@RejectTestId"
                       OnClick="() => OnReject.InvokeAsync(Proposal)">@RejectLabel</AppButton>
        </div>
    }
</article>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public AiProposal Proposal { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Whether to show what would be replaced. On by default, because
    /// without it accepting is a gamble rather than a review — but an Answer
    /// replaces nothing, and a "Now" heading over an empty block would be the
    /// card inventing a before that never existed.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowOriginal { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>"Use this" rather than "Accept". Accept reads as agreeing with
    /// the machine; the reader is choosing a wording, not conceding a point.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string AcceptLabel { get; set; } = "Use this";

    [Parameter]
    public string RejectLabel { get; set; } = "Discard";

    /// <summary>Raised with the whole proposal. The host applies it and hands
    /// back new content; nothing here writes into a document, for the same reason
    /// MarkdownView's OnEditComment raises the record rather than storing it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<AiProposal> OnAccept { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<AiProposal> OnReject { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? AttributionTestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? OriginalTestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? BodyTestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? AcceptTestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? RejectTestId { get; set; }

    private string ProviderName => IntegrationStates.NameOf(Proposal.Provider);

    private string StateLabel => Proposal.State switch
    {
        AiProposalState.Accepted => "Used",
        AiProposalState.Rejected => "Discarded",
        _ => string.Empty
    };

    private string ClassValue
    {
        get
        {
            var stem = "ai-proposal "
                     + $"ai-proposal--{IntegrationStates.SlugOf(Proposal.Provider)} "
                     + $"ai-proposal--{Proposal.State.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()}";

            return string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? stem : $"{stem} {CssClass}";
        }
    }
}
